DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
Analytical Marxism emerged in the late 1970s as a self-consciously rigorous
attempt to reconstruct Marxist theory using the tools of analytic philosophy,
rational choice theory, and game theory. The founding moment is typically
dated to G.A. Cohen's Karl Marx's Theory of History: A Defence (1978), which
argued that historical materialism is best interpreted as a form of technological
determinism in which productive forces have primacy over relations of production,
and that the relations of production can be explained functionally by their
tendency to develop those forces. Cohen mobilized the resources of analytic
philosophy to give historical materialism a rigorous formulation — what he called
a "restricted" or "pure" version of the theory.

The central debate between Cohen and Jon Elster concerns functional explanation.
Cohen defended the legitimacy of functional or "consequence explanations" in
Marxism, arguing that a phenomenon can be explained by its consequences even
without a known feedback mechanism. Elster, by contrast, argued from a
methodological individualist standpoint that functional explanations are
inadmissible unless supported by an explicit causal mechanism — typically a
selection process or intentional action. In Making Sense of Marx (1985), Elster
proposed rational choice theory and game theory as the proper foundations for
Marxist social science. This methodological dispute ramified into substantive
disagreements about teleology, mechanism, and the role of individual agency in
historical explanation. John Roemer contributed a rigorous neoclassical analysis
of exploitation and class in A General Theory of Exploitation and Class (1982),
while Erik Olin Wright developed a neo-Marxist account of contradictory class
locations in Classes (1985).

By the early 1990s, the movement had begun to fragment: Elster and Adam
Przeworski departed from the September Group in 1993, with Elster eventually
concluding that analytical scrutiny had exposed Marxism as largely empty. The
movement's legacy remains contested: critics (Ellen Meiksins Wood, Alex
Callinicos) argued it transformed Marxism into its opposite by abandoning
dialectical method and class-struggle agency; defenders maintained that rigorous
theory is prerequisite to emancipatory politics.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
This domain is directly central to a project on Marx's philosophy and theory of
history, as Analytical Marxism constitutes the most sustained attempt to
reconstruct and test historical materialism's core claims (primacy of productive
forces, functional explanation of the superstructure, exploitation and class)
using contemporary philosophical standards. The Cohen-Elster debate represents
the sharpest recent confrontation between different interpretations of what kind
of explanation historical materialism provides and what assumptions it requires.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
The reception of Analytical Marxism in non-Anglophone traditions is
underrepresented. Recent (post-2010) reassessments of whether "restricted"
historical materialism can survive the critiques of both Elster and feminist
scholars like Casal remain scattered across journals and are not easily surveyed.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
The Cohen-Elster methodological dispute should be framed as a focal point that
connects to broader questions about functional explanation, teleology, and
mechanism in Marx's philosophy of history. Wright and Roemer represent
applications of the programme to class and exploitation; critics represent the
external debate about whether the analytical framework distorts Marxism.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Cohen's Reconstructed Functionalism: 4 papers - Defends functional explanation
  and technological primacy as the core of historical materialism
- Elster's Methodological Individualism: 3 papers - Rejects functional
  explanation; insists on microfoundations via rational choice and game theory
- Roemer/Wright Neoclassical Reconstruction: 3 papers - Applies formal economic
  methods to exploitation and class analysis
- Critical Reception: 5 papers - External critiques arguing Analytical Marxism
  deforms or abandons Marxism
====================================================================
}

@book{cohen1978kmth,
