  technological determinism: productive forces (tools, skills, knowledge) have
  primacy over relations of production in that they explain why relations take
  the form they do. The relations are explained functionally — they persist
  because they facilitate the development of the forces. Cohen distinguishes a
  "restricted" historical materialism (about forces/relations) from an
  "inclusive" version covering ideology and the state, and defends only the
  former with rigorous arguments drawn from analytic philosophy and philosophy
  of science.

  RELEVANCE: This is the founding text of Analytical Marxism and the direct
  reference point for the Cohen-Elster debate. For a project on Marx's theory
  of history, it provides both the most rigorous modern reconstruction of
  historical materialism and the benchmark against which all subsequent
  analytical-Marxist work is measured. Cohen's formulation of the "development
  thesis" and "primacy thesis" are the core interpretive claims at stake.

  POSITION: Represents the reconstructed technological-determinist reading of
  historical materialism; foundational for the entire Analytical Marxism movement.
  },
