  RELEVANCE: Provides an accessible entry point into Roemer's program and its
  implications for understanding the economic mechanisms that historical
  materialism posits. The reframing of exploitation as asset inequality connects
  the economic side of Analytical Marxism to normative political philosophy.

  POSITION: Represents the transition in Roemer's work from technical economic
  theory to normative theory of exploitation and socialist justice.
  },
  keywords = {analytical-marxism, exploitation, class, Roemer, political-economy, Medium},
  abstract = {Roemer challenges the morality of an economic system based on private ownership of the means of production. Unless you start with a certain amount of wealth in such a society, you are only "free to lose." This book addresses crucial questions of political philosophy and normative economics.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{levine1985historical,
  author = {Levine, Andrew and Sober, Elliott},
  title = {What's Historical about Historical Materialism?},
  journal = {The Journal of Philosophy},
  year = {1985},
  volume = {82},
  number = {6},
  pages = {304--326},
  doi = {10.2307/2026564},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Levine and Sober argue that Darwinian evolutionary theory and
  historical materialism represent fundamentally different strategies for making
  history an object of theoretical investigation. Evolutionary theory abstracts
  away from specific historical trajectories; historical materialism, by contrast,
  purports to identify directional historical laws. They scrutinize what kind of
  explanation Cohen's primacy thesis actually provides and whether evolutionary
  analogy can underwrite the functional explanations at the core of historical
  materialism without collapsing into mere narrative.

  RELEVANCE: This article clarifies the structure of historical explanation in
  Marxism by comparing it with evolutionary biology — directly relevant to
  questions about teleology and directionality in Marx's theory of history.
  It also engages the Cohen-Elster dispute from a philosophy of science perspective.

  POSITION: Philosophy-of-science contribution to the Analytical Marxism debate,
  distinguishing historical-materialist from evolutionary explanatory strategies.
  },
  keywords = {analytical-marxism, historical-explanation, philosophy-of-science, Levine, Sober, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{wright1992reconstructing,
  author = {Wright, Erik Olin and Levine, Andrew and Sober, Elliott},
  title = {Reconstructing Marxism: Essays on Explanation and the Theory of History},
  year = {1992},
  publisher = {Verso},
  address = {London},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: A collaborative volume by three Analytical Marxists that
  reassesses the movement's achievements and limitations in reconstructing
  historical materialism. The essays address the relationship between functional
  explanation and evolutionary selection, the status of the primacy thesis after
  two decades of debate, and the viability of a "reconstructed" Marxism that
  retains the core commitment to understanding social transformation while
  shedding untenable metaphysical claims. Wright, Levine, and Sober argue for
  a more modest but defensible version of historical materialism that abandons
  strict technological determinism.

  RELEVANCE: This represents the mature analytical-Marxist reconsideration of
  Cohen's original programme after the Elster critique. It directly addresses
  how much of historical materialism can survive rigorous scrutiny — central to
  the project's question about the fate of the theory.

  POSITION: Internal revision of Analytical Marxism; represents the post-debate
  consolidation that modifies rather than abandons the historical-materialist
  framework.
  },
  keywords = {analytical-marxism, historical-materialism, reconstruction, Wright, Levine, Sober, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{carling1993evolutionary,
  author = {Carling, Alan},
  title = {Analytical Marxism and Historical Materialism: The Debate on Social Evolution},
  journal = {Science \& Society: A Journal of Marxist Thought and Analysis},
  year = {1993},
  volume = {57},
  number = {1},
  pages = {31--65},
  doi = {10.1177/003682379305700103},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Carling distinguishes three doctrines of primacy in historical
  materialism — Natural Primacy (true but limited), Intentional Primacy (Cohen's
  version, untenable), and Competitive Primacy (a genuinely evolutionary doctrine
  that could underpin functional explanations). The argument is that Cohen's
  "intentional primacy" — the claim that productive forces develop because agents
  intentionally act to develop them — is both empirically dubious and logically
  different from the evolutionary selection mechanism that would genuinely
  vindicate functional explanation. Competitive Primacy, grounded in competition
  between social formations, better matches the Marxian theory.

  RELEVANCE: Carling's tripartite distinction clarifies what is actually at stake
  in the Cohen-Elster debate about primacy and functional explanation. For a
  project on Marx's philosophy of history, this provides a more fine-grained
  taxonomy of the explanatory options than either Cohen or Elster offers.

  POSITION: Internal reconstruction of the primacy thesis within Analytical
  Marxism; proposes evolutionary selection as the proper mechanism for historical
  materialism.
  },
  keywords = {analytical-marxism, primacy-thesis, historical-materialism, Carling, functional-explanation, Medium},
  abstract = {Darwinian evolutionary theory marries a genetic account of the origin of species to a selectionist account of their subsequent fate. The Marxian theory of history is analogous: class struggle provides an account of the origins of new regimes of production and selection pressures explain their subsequent history. The theory is technologically determinist, but there are three distinct doctrines ascribing primacy in different ways to the technological forces of production over the social relations of production. Natural Primacy is probably true but of limited relevance to the debate about historical process. Intentional Primacy, as advocated by G. A. Cohen, is untenable. Competitive Primacy is a genuinely evolutionary doctrine that may even be true.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{veneziani2012analytical,
  author = {Veneziani, Roberto},
  title = {Analytical Marxism},
  journal = {Journal of Economic Surveys},
  year = {2012},
  volume = {26},
  number = {4},
  pages = {649--673},
  doi = {10.1111/j.1467-6419.2010.00667.x},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: A comprehensive survey of the Analytical Marxism literature
  that distinguishes the movement from "Rational Choice Marxism" and provides
  a systematic account of its contributions across historical materialism,
  exploitation theory, class analysis, and normative political philosophy.
  Veneziani argues that an anti-reductionist version of Analytical Marxism —
  one that takes endogenous preferences and structural constraints seriously —
  can constitute a distinctive and productive research programme in explanatory
  social theory beyond the movement's classic phase.

  RELEVANCE: This survey provides orientation for a research project by mapping
  the range of Analytical Marxist contributions and identifying what remains
  defensible after internal and external critique. It is the most recent
  comprehensive overview of the field's scope and legacy.

  POSITION: Contemporary overview of the Analytical Marxism tradition and
  reassessment of its viability as an ongoing research programme.
  },
  keywords = {analytical-marxism, survey, exploitation, class, Veneziani, Medium},
  abstract = {Abstract This paper provides a comprehensive survey of the literature on Analytical Marxism (AM) and analyses its relevance for social theory. AM is precisely defined and distinguished from Rational Choice Marxism (RCM). The different substantive implications of the two approaches are discussed: according to RCM, the role of Marxism in the social sciences is exhausted, whereas AM has reconstructed a set of propositions that aim to provide the foundations of a distinctive approach in social theory. The methodological debate around and within AM is analysed and the shortcomings of methodological individualism and rational choice theory are stressed, raising doubts on the claim that RCM is the only scientific approach to Marx's theory. Yet, wholesale rejections on a priori methodological or exegetical grounds are questioned. A focused, immanent critique of RCM is developed, which emphasizes the lack of an adequate analysis of structural constraints and endogenous preferences in RCM models. It is argued that the analysis of endogenous preferences and structural constraints is consistent with an anti‐reductionist AM approach and it may provide the outline of a fertile, distinctive research programme in explanatory social theory that builds on the core of Marxism identified by AM.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{tarrit2006brief,
  author = {Tarrit, Fabien},
  title = {A Brief History, Scope, and Peculiarities of ``Analytical Marxism''},
  journal = {Review of Radical Political Economics},
  year = {2006},
  volume = {38},
  number = {4},
  pages = {595--618},
  doi = {10.1177/0486613406293223},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Tarrit provides an intellectual history of Analytical Marxism
  tracing its three main contributions — Cohen's defence of historical materialism
  using analytic philosophy, Roemer's reconstruction of Marxian economics with
  neoclassical tools, and Elster's interpretation of Marxism through methodological
  individualism and rational choice theory. Tarrit argues that Analytical Marxism
  effectively turned Marxism into its opposite by abandoning the dialectical method
  and class-struggle perspective, substituting methodological individualism for
  the analysis of collective historical agency.

  RELEVANCE: Tarrit offers a critical historical overview that situates Analytical
  Marxism's contribution and its limitations. For the project on Marx's theory of
  history, this provides context on how the analytical reconstruction was received
  from within radical political economy and why the movement's self-critique
  intensified from the 1990s onward.

  POSITION: Critical external assessment of Analytical Marxism from the perspective
  of radical political economy; argues the analytical project distorted Marxism.
  },
  keywords = {analytical-marxism, history-of-ideas, critique, Tarrit, Low},
  abstract = {The issue of the article is to describe and evaluate analytical Marxism, a school of thought that occupies a particular position in radical theory. It has presented itself as an attempt to renew Marxism with the tools of non-Marxist philosophy (analytical philosophy), epistemology (logical positivism), and social science (methodological individualism). The main authors are Gerald A. Cohen, who defended historical materialism on the basis of analytical philosophy; John Roemer, who reconstructed Marxian economics with neoclassical tools; and Jon Elster, who interpreted Marxism with methodological individualism. Rational choice theory plays an important role in analytical Marxism. This article deals with the idea that analytical Marxism seems to have turned Marxism into its opposite.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{roemer1982new,
  author = {Roemer, John E.},
  title = {New Directions in the Marxian Theory of Exploitation and Classes},
  journal = {Politics \& Society},
  year = {1982},
  volume = {11},
  number = {3},
  pages = {253--287},
  doi = {10.1177/003232928201100302},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Roemer sketches the axiomatic approach to exploitation that he
  develops fully in A General Theory. He argues that exploitation can be defined
  as unequal exchange without the labor theory of value, using the concept of
  "socially necessary labor" grounded in asset inequality. The article introduces
  the "Class-Exploitation Correspondence Principle" and discusses the implications
  of different types of productive asset inequality (physical capital, skills,
  organizational assets) for the class structure of different social formations
  including feudalism, capitalism, and socialism.

  RELEVANCE: This article is the programmatic statement of Roemer's analytical
  approach to exploitation and class, making the key methodological moves
  explicit in a more accessible format than the monograph. It directly addresses
  the economic foundations of historical materialism's account of class society.

  POSITION: Programmatic statement of Roemer's neoclassical reconstruction of
  Marxian exploitation and class theory.
  },
  keywords = {analytical-marxism, exploitation, class, Roemer, political-economy, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{wright1989comparative,
  author = {Wright, Erik Olin},
  title = {The Comparative Project on Class Structure and Class Consciousness: An Overview},
  journal = {Acta Sociologica},
  year = {1989},
  volume = {32},
  number = {1},
  pages = {3--22},
  doi = {10.1177/000169938903200101},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Wright reports the methodology and preliminary findings of the
  Comparative Class Structure Project — a large-scale cross-national survey
  research programme applying his neo-Marxist class categories empirically.
  The article explains how the contradictory class locations framework and the
  exploitation-based class map can be operationalized for survey research, and
  presents early comparative data on class structure in the United States,
  Sweden, and several other countries. Wright addresses the relationship between
  objective class position and class consciousness.

  RELEVANCE: This article shows the empirical program that emerged from the
  Analytical Marxist reconstruction of class theory, directly connecting the
  abstract framework of Cohen, Roemer, and Wright's theoretical work to
  sociological research. It illustrates the ambition of Analytical Marxism to
  bridge philosophy and empirical social science.

  POSITION: Empirical application of the Analytical Marxist class framework;
  represents Wright's attempt to make the theory of class testable.
  },
  keywords = {analytical-marxism, class, sociology, Wright, empirical, Low},
  abstract = {The Comparative Project on class structure and class consciousness began in 1977 as an attempt to remedy what I felt was a serious shortcoming in the Marxist tradition of research within sociology. Until very recently empirical investigations within the Marxist tradition have been almost exclusively restricted to historical and qualitative research. This is to be explained by a variety of factors: the general hostility of radical scholars in general to 'positivism'; the particular substantive concerns of Marxists with broad historical dynamics; the general hostility of academic programs that stress quantitative methods to the kinds of theoretical concerns and political commitments of Marxist students. Whatever the explanation, however, Marxist intellectuals have generally not pursued quantitative strategies of research. As a graduate student in sociology in the early 1970s I decided that it was important for Marxism to engage in serious and systematic quantitative research. On the one hand, I felt that if Marxism was to be taken seriously within US sociology, it was necessary to demonstrate its explanatory power on the central terrain of academic sociology, which is clearly quantitative research. This does not mean that quantification should preempt other lines of research, nor that it should be treated as some kind of privileged basis for developing and reconstructing theoretical arguments. But simply to abandon that terrain completely risked perpetuating the marginality of Marxism within the academy. On the other hand, quite apart from problems of strengthening the academic legitimacy of Marxism, I felt that Marxism itself had much to gain from statistical research, both because of the conceptual discipline it imposes on the clarification of arguments (so that they can be operationalized), and because at least some of the important questions which are traditionally asked by Marxists can be illuminated through multivariate empirical strategies. One problem faced by an aspiring 'multivariate Marxist', however, was the lack of suitable data for such research. All of the already-existing datasets of which I was aware had been gathered by scholars working in other theoretical traditions,},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}
